00:10:05.793 --> 00:10:07.561
I won't say DRock, are you feeling,

00:10:07.561 --> 00:10:09.982
oh you're feeling uneasy
about this wine, cool,

00:10:09.982 --> 00:10:11.259
let's go in a different direction.

00:10:11.259 --> 00:10:13.975
Maybe I have to ask that
a little bit more tangibly

00:10:13.975 --> 00:10:16.867
black and white in a
conversation on digital,

00:10:16.867 --> 00:10:19.639
but it's the same effort, same mentality,

00:10:19.639 --> 00:10:23.738
and the beauty is emojis
and short form and slang

00:10:23.738 --> 00:10:26.977
have given more context around
the written word online.

00:10:26.977 --> 00:10:28.360
We, as human beings,

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:29.636
are great at communicating.

00:10:29.636 --> 00:10:31.922
People grossly underestimate our ability

00:10:31.922 --> 00:10:32.974
to be communicators.

00:10:32.974 --> 00:10:35.670
Whether drawing on caves
or making smoke signals

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:37.761
or radio television, the written word,

00:10:37.761 --> 00:10:41.713
the Internet, commenting, emojis,

00:10:41.713 --> 00:10:43.322
we're talented at this.

00:10:43.322 --> 00:10:45.982
I'm watching all of us evolve.

00:10:45.982 --> 00:10:50.253
Very many of us, many of you
who have been romantic about

00:10:50.253 --> 00:10:52.726
grammar, have finally let it go.

00:10:53.486 --> 00:10:55.976
All of us are misspelling words on purpose

00:10:55.976 --> 00:10:57.056
so it auto-corrects,

00:10:57.056 --> 00:10:58.739
because we value the speed.

00:10:58.739 --> 00:11:00.711
We're using emojis,

00:11:00.711 --> 00:11:03.813
not only 13 year olds
or just people in Asia,

00:11:03.813 --> 00:11:07.188
now it is a worldwide phenomenon.

00:11:07.188 --> 00:11:09.107
We're evolving and we're great at it,

00:11:09.107 --> 00:11:13.224
so I look for those cues
and keep trying to evolve

00:11:13.224 --> 00:11:16.189
and stay ahead of where I
think we're all evolving to.

00:11:16.971 --> 00:11:20.066
- Hey Gary, Ryan here
from onproperty.com.au,

00:11:20.066 --> 00:11:22.378
just want to say thank you
because your book, Crush It!,

00:11:22.378 --> 00:11:24.769
allowed me to go out full time on my own.

00:11:24.769 --> 00:11:27.071
My question is why did you
start a service business,

00:11:27.071 --> 00:11:30.252
your agency, instead of
starting a media company,

00:11:30.252 --> 00:11:32.008
which you seem really good at?

00:11:32.008 --> 00:11:33.712
- Ryan, great question.

00:11:33.712 --> 00:11:34.616
I mean this is...

00:11:35.304 --> 00:11:36.350
Can we get like a,

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:38.296
Can you make some sort an all time great

00:11:38.296 --> 00:11:39.779
episode alert thing here?

00:11:39.779 --> 00:11:41.064
Put it in my hand.

00:11:41.064 --> 00:11:42.905
(majestic music)

00:11:42.905 --> 00:11:44.458
This is a great question

00:11:44.458 --> 00:11:47.256
and it's a very specific, detailed answer

00:11:47.256 --> 00:11:48.520
for a couple reasons.

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:51.993
One, I'm not sure that I
was aware of how good I was

00:11:51.993 --> 00:11:54.012
at it five years ago.

00:11:54.012 --> 00:11:57.553
Two, I had an inkling when
we started VaynerMedia.

00:11:57.553 --> 00:11:59.584
The first project we worked
on was something called

00:11:59.584 --> 00:12:01.481
Daily1to10.com

00:12:01.481 --> 00:12:05.000
and we were doing Facebook
fan pages driving to a Tumblr.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:09.363
So, I was on that kick from the get,

00:12:09.363 --> 00:12:12.017
but three, the most practical thing was,

00:12:12.017 --> 00:12:15.542
I knew that speed mattered
and that money mattered,

00:12:15.542 --> 00:12:19.012
and that scale mattered
and the agency-client

00:12:19.012 --> 00:12:22.098
service business was very obvious to me

00:12:22.098 --> 00:12:25.465
as the quickest path to scale and money.

00:12:25.465 --> 00:12:26.763
Because now we have Vayner Publishing

00:12:26.763 --> 00:12:28.639
and we're building out lostlettermen.com,

00:12:28.639 --> 00:12:30.214
you can check it out, we just bought it,

00:12:30.214 --> 00:12:32.789
wait for the redesign, but
we're now staring to get into

00:12:32.789 --> 00:12:34.714
the media business and we
have the infrastructure

00:12:34.714 --> 00:12:36.264
and the scale and the skill set.

00:12:36.264 --> 00:12:38.039
And I have 500 people that
I've been able to effect

00:12:38.039 --> 00:12:39.414
with my thinking.

00:12:39.414 --> 00:12:41.204
They've got to counter that
with their own thinking,

00:12:41.204 --> 00:12:43.775
but clearly, as a CEO and a
thought leader in a space,

00:12:43.775 --> 00:12:45.809
I'm sure I'm evolving their
thinking towards a direction

00:12:45.809 --> 00:12:47.503
that picks up speed.

00:12:47.503 --> 00:12:48.572
Because the more I agree with them,

00:12:48.572 --> 00:12:50.062
and they agree with me,

00:12:50.062 --> 00:12:52.157
the quicker we can be
at what we need to do.

00:12:52.157 --> 00:12:54.806
So, really, the answer
to your question is,

00:12:54.806 --> 00:12:56.373
because I'm patient.

00:12:56.373 --> 00:12:58.544
I thought the right
strategy was to build out

00:12:58.544 --> 00:13:00.750
an infrastructure that
allowed me to get to it later.

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:02.376
Plus, not to mention,

00:13:02.376 --> 00:13:04.847
media's evolved enormously
in those five years.

00:13:04.847 --> 00:13:07.997
And if you look at media
and the upside of media,

00:13:07.997 --> 00:13:10.304
and the media sites of today,

00:13:10.304 --> 00:13:14.402
five years ago they were very
predicated on SEO and SEM,

00:13:14.402 --> 00:13:16.167
something that I never really loved.

00:13:16.167 --> 00:13:19.406
Now, we're predicated more on
social sharing and content,

00:13:19.406 --> 00:13:20.729
something I love more.

00:13:20.729 --> 00:13:24.191
So, I think that, my intuition,

00:13:24.191 --> 00:13:25.917
and I'd like to think rightfully so,

00:13:25.917 --> 00:13:30.005
at the time was, I was not the
perfect player for that time.

00:13:30.005 --> 00:13:32.860
But my intuition was, there's
a chance that the market may

00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:33.789
move into that,

00:13:33.789 --> 00:13:35.925
if I'm in a certain place
in five years to capture

00:13:35.925 --> 00:13:38.885
the market's evolution on
the media side perfectly,

00:13:38.885 --> 00:13:41.045
then I can win triple.

00:13:41.045 --> 00:13:43.649
And I think I'm starting to maybe play out

00:13:43.649 --> 00:13:45.421
and benefit on that bet.

00:13:45.421 --> 00:13:47.383
So, I was just strategic about it.

00:13:48.305 --> 00:13:49.268
- [Voiceover] Antoine asks,

00:13:49.268 --> 00:13:52.100
"What usually prompts you
to walk away or turn down

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:53.975
"great opportunities?"

00:13:55.468 --> 00:13:57.327
- Well, Antoine, I don't
want to be in the business

00:13:57.327 --> 00:13:59.346
of saying no to great opportunities.

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:02.252
I'd like to think that the
answer to the question is

00:14:02.252 --> 00:14:03.367
my intuition.

00:14:03.367 --> 00:14:06.989
I'd like to think my intuition
is allowing me to walk away

00:14:06.989 --> 00:14:10.701
from bad opportunities that
may look good on paper.

00:14:12.392 --> 00:14:15.135
You know, I just go with my intuition.

00:14:15.135 --> 00:14:16.351
Really nothing else.

00:14:16.351 --> 00:14:18.232
I mean, I'm not an analytical thinker.

00:14:18.232 --> 00:14:19.033
Right?

00:14:19.033 --> 00:14:20.938
I'm not going to look
at a whole lot of data.

00:14:20.938 --> 00:14:23.974
A lot of these big funds
that try to ask me like,

00:14:23.974 --> 00:14:25.650
well how did I know this
startup was going to be great

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:28.057
or why did I think this
was going to be great,

00:14:28.057 --> 00:14:30.549
it was never predicated
on monthly active users

00:14:30.549 --> 00:14:32.186
or some sort of other data set.

00:14:32.186 --> 00:14:34.589
It was me, what I would like
to say is that I like to

00:14:34.589 --> 00:14:35.795
taste things.

00:14:35.795 --> 00:14:37.305
It's me observing

00:14:37.305 --> 00:14:40.285
and then me counter
punching that observation.

00:14:40.285 --> 00:14:43.226
I tend to be very, very
all-in on my intuition,

00:14:43.226 --> 00:14:47.007
and so that's, you know,

00:14:47.007 --> 00:14:48.823
that's kind of how I make the calls.

00:14:49.393 --> 00:14:51.607
It's funny the way you asked the question.

00:14:51.607 --> 00:14:53.574
You asked the question in
what I think is more of

00:14:53.574 --> 00:14:55.014
a defensive mindset,

00:14:55.014 --> 00:14:57.115
which is if you breakdown the question,

00:14:57.115 --> 00:14:59.585
it ends with the real kicker which is,

00:14:59.585 --> 00:15:01.873
run it one more time for
me, India, how do you--

00:15:02.124 --> 00:15:04.062
- [India] How do you,
what usually prompts you

00:15:04.062 --> 00:15:06.567
to walk away from or turn
down or know when to say no

00:15:06.567 --> 00:15:07.778
to a great opportunity?

00:15:07.778 --> 00:15:09.534
- To me that never runs through my mind.

00:15:09.534 --> 00:15:11.866
The thought of saying no
to great opportunities

00:15:11.866 --> 00:15:14.041
is just not in, I don't
put those words in that

00:15:14.041 --> 00:15:15.696
pattern of a sentence.

00:15:16.356 --> 00:15:18.760
So, that is what I think you
should be thinking about.

00:15:18.760 --> 00:15:22.212
It sounds to me that you're
crippled by the miss.

00:15:22.212 --> 00:15:23.276
I'm not.

00:15:23.276 --> 00:15:26.215
I just keep having the
at bats and the swings,

00:15:26.215 --> 00:15:28.882
and I know that I'll
have more wins than loses

00:15:28.882 --> 00:15:30.138
and if I pass on something good,

00:15:30.138 --> 00:15:31.492
so be it.

00:15:31.492 --> 00:15:33.200
But literally, I mean,
we've talked about it

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:34.200
on this show before,

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:36.626
passing on Uber's angel round
twice and leaving $300 million

00:15:36.626 --> 00:15:38.133
dollars on the table.

00:15:38.133 --> 00:15:41.165
Literally, outside of being
a great story to tell you

00:15:41.165 --> 00:15:44.346
how I feel, doesn't
come into my mind ever.

00:15:44.946 --> 00:15:46.574
Well, maybe once in a blue moon.

00:15:46.574 --> 00:15:48.374
But, pretty much never, and I think

00:15:48.374 --> 00:15:49.884
that's an important thing.

00:15:49.884 --> 00:15:52.588
And an offensive mindset
versus a defensive mindset,

00:15:52.588 --> 00:15:55.525
something I want to get
deeper into content team.

00:15:56.942 --> 00:15:57.911
- [India] That's everything.

00:15:57.911 --> 00:15:59.205
- Cool.

00:16:00.436 --> 00:16:04.220
Question of the day,
what is the biggest miss

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:05.056
of your career?

00:16:05.056 --> 00:16:06.577
What is the one thing you said no to

00:16:06.577 --> 00:16:07.657
that weren't that great?

00:16:07.657 --> 00:16:09.802
I had a meeting the other
day with a woman who passed

00:16:09.802 --> 00:16:13.203
on being one of the first 600
or 500 employees at Google.

00:16:13.203 --> 00:16:15.134
She felt pretty down about it.

00:16:16.809 --> 00:16:18.532
So, I'm fascinated by that.

00:16:18.532 --> 00:16:20.308
So, give me your answer.

00:16:20.308 --> 00:16:22.255
You keep asking questions,

00:16:22.255 --> 00:16:24.237
I'll keep answering them.

00:16:24.237 --> 00:16:26.794
Much love, thank you so much for watching.

00:16:26.794 --> 00:16:28.072
Hope you enjoyed it.

00:16:28.897 --> 00:16:30.587
He's a Jets fan, he's used to losing.

00:16:30.587 --> 00:16:31.463
That hurts.

00:16:31.463 --> 00:16:33.099
Alright, see ya.

00:16:33.099 --> 00:16:35.295
(laughter)

